						<Item class="StringValue" referent="973">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.selection

VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.selection option values and behavior.

- `selection` is a writable key-set map.
- `selectionMode` defaults to single when you supply `selection` or its
  callback. Otherwise it defaults to none.
- `onSelectionChange(nextMap)`, `onActivate(item, key)` and `onReachEnd`
  connect control events to domain behavior.
- With `onSelectionChange`, the caller must write `selection:set(nextMap)` to
  accept the proposal. Without it, the collection updates the selection cell.
- Outside Edit mode, when the selection mode is not `none`, one mouse click selects a row. A
  double click, Return, a gamepad press or a touch tap runs `onActivate`. A
  double click keeps the selection. When the mode is `none`, each activation
  runs `onActivate`. Table rows follow the same rule.
- In Edit mode with selection enabled, activation changes eligible row selection instead of
  calling `onActivate`. Selection is not a game commitment such as Buy or Equip.
- A selected row paints the theme's selected color. A [Card](Card.title.md)
  row shows it on the card's own plate, and the collection paints no plate
  under the card.
- `selectable(item)`, `reorderable`, `movable(item)`, `dragLabel` and
  `onReorder(keys, insertionSlot)` use the same zero-based insertion contract
  among the remaining rows as Table.
- A keyboard or gamepad move steps along the scrolling axis. A vertical
  collection uses Up and Down. A horizontal collection uses Left and Right.
- PageDown and PageUp, or RT and LT on a gamepad, move about one viewport at
  a time and skip disabled and collapsed rows. A grid pages within the
  column; from the row above a short last row, a page reaches the last item.
  Home and End reach the first and last row. While a row holds the selection,
  the collection takes RT and LT, so a game that uses the triggers releases
  the selection before play resumes.
- A reorder that would move a row that is not movable does not occur.

Native properties and children stay available.

In strict Luau, give the spec a type on a local and pass the local to the
constructor. `Facet.KeyedCollectionSpec<Item, Key>` is a spec with `key`.
`Facet.IdentityCollectionSpec<Item>` is a spec without `key`. Outline and
Table use `Facet.KeyedOutlineSpec`, `Facet.IdentityOutlineSpec`,
`Facet.KeyedTableSpec` and `Facet.IdentityTableSpec`. The type gives `render`,
`current` and `use` their types, so they need no annotation. A key function, a
positional child such as `UI.fill()` or an unannotated `render` in an inline
spec makes the type checker report `None of the overloads ... are compatible`
without the cause. Put the type on the spec to see the cause.

```luau
type Item = { id: string, title: string }

local rows = Compose.cell({ { id = "a", title = "Amber" } } :: { Item })
local spec: Facet.KeyedCollectionSpec<Item, string> = {
    UI.fill(),
    from = rows,
    key = function(item, _index) return item.id end,
    itemSize = 52,
    render = function(current)
        return UI.Button {
            label = function(use) return use(current).title end,
            onActivate = function() inspect(current:peek().id) end,
            Size = UDim2.new(1, 0, 0, 52),
        }
    end,
}
local list = UI.VirtualList "Rows" (spec)
```
]]></string>
								<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
								<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
								<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
								<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
								<string name="Name">VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.selection.md</string>
								<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
								<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
								<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
								<UniqueId name="UniqueId">73c8afe78d24b4d30afced11000007aa</UniqueId>
							</Properties>
						</Item>
						<Item class="StringValue" referent="974">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.sizing

For a short, bounded collection inside a content-sized surface, such as a set
of upgrade cards in a `hug` Sheet, use `mode = "all"` and `hug = true`. The
collection then takes the height of its rows (the width of its columns on a
horizontal list), including its paint gutters, and does not scroll. An empty
collection keeps its layout and refills.

On a VirtualGrid, give `minColumnWidth` rather than `columns`, and let the grid
own the width. The available width sets the column count. With `hug`, a grid
never makes more columns than it has items, so three cards share a wide panel
with no empty column at the end, and they wrap into rows on a narrow screen. Do
not compute item sizes or columns from the viewport or the outer width in game
code: the scrollbar inset, the paint gutters and the surface padding all
reduce the space for columns, and a size clamped to a fixed range leaves dead
space beside the items when the surface is wider than they are.

Objects that players compare need at least two columns on a portrait phone.
A phone leaves about 340 pixels for the grid, so a `minColumnWidth` above
about 160 gives one column there. Add `minColumns = 2`: the columns then become
narrower than `minColumnWidth` before the grid drops below two.

```luau
local Facet = require(game.ReplicatedStorage.Packages.Facet)
local app = Facet.app({})
local UI = app.UI
app.mount(function()
  local env = UI.environment()
  local spec: Facet.KeyedCollectionSpec<string, string> = {
    from = { "Amber", "Blue", "Coral" }, key = function(item) return item end,
    mode = "all", hug = true, measure = true,
    minColumnWidth = env.scaled(200), itemSize = env.scaled(132), gap = env.scaled(16),
    render = function(current) return UI.Text { text = current } end,
  }
  return UI.VirtualGrid(spec)
end)
```

`hug` requires `mode = "all"`; a windowed collection refuses it. For large or
unbounded collections, keep `mode = "windowed"`, give the viewport a bounded
height and let the collection scroll. Do not size it to all items.
`AutomaticCanvasSize` changes canvas size, not viewport size; native child
measurement cannot measure unmounted rows.
]]></string>
								<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
								<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
								<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
								<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
								<string name="Name">VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.sizing.md</string>
								<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
								<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
								<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
								<UniqueId name="UniqueId">73c8afe78d24b4d30afced11000007ab</UniqueId>
							</Properties>
						</Item>
						<Item class="StringValue" referent="975">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# VirtualList.card

Show a virtual row collection.

Inside an app mount, set `local UI = app.UI`.

```luau
local spec: Facet.KeyedCollectionSpec<string, string> = { from = { "Kart" }, key = function(item) return item end, itemSize = 44, render = function(current) return UI.Text { text = current } end }
UI.VirtualList "Rows" (spec)
```

Key options: `from`, `key`, `render`, `itemSize`, `measure`.

Give the viewport a bounded parent.

Related controls: [Controls](Controls.md).

Open:

- [VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid](VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.md)
]]></string>
								<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
