						<Item class="StringValue" referent="1071">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Compose within narrow and short room

Portrait can support a vertical subject-to-action sequence. Short height often
needs subject and decision side by side, fewer fixed rows, or a separate step.
Use the actual container after native controls, navigation and keyboard. Preserve
world visibility when it communicates identity or the decision; it does not
replace a recognizable subject and clear action.

## Build the first useful view

Render with realistic labels, required controls, data and game art. On entry,
show useful content and the next action without scrolling past setup. Long
catalogs still scroll; not every record must fit at once.

| Task | Portrait allocation | Short-height allocation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Enter an activity | Compact identity, recognizable selected subject, concise invitation and primary action. Secondary destinations follow or use navigation. | Subject beside invitation/action, or a world-led edge composition. Keep the primary route visible without a tall introductory stack. |
| Browse a collection | One title/Back owner, search plus Filters access, visible records with decisive values. Open the selected detail as a complete step. | A compact search/filter row above useful records. Show a detail step unless both panes leave enough content room. Do not stack every filter above the first item. |
| Inspect or customize | Selected subject followed closely by decisive facts and action; supporting history/choices scroll. Show selected colors or materials visually. | Subject beside a bounded decision column. The inspection example demonstrates this relationship. Do not retain a tall portrait preview above the action. |
| Read a result | Outcome, accepted gains and Continue form the first group. Standings and progression follow in a bounded body. | Outcome/gains beside Continue, with supporting comparison below or disclosed. Do not require a long podium list before reaching the return action. |
| Act during play | Brief readouts, a clear view ahead, controls grouped by simultaneous hand use. | Reduce permanent labels and status rows; keep native movement areas and the subject clear. A touch pad is not a universal pointer/controller form. |
| Learn an action | Callout near the live target with concise lesson and separate step controls. | Compact contextual guidance that leaves the target usable and visible. A floating lesson keeps outer space and footer padding. |

Keep requirements available. Filters uses the same query/category/sort model;
show active filters and result count. Reset/Clear retain their specified meaning.
Price, consequence, pending/error state and required terms stay with the action.

## Allocate the remaining room

Give title and Back one owner: NavBar, page or Sheet header. Measure what remains
after navigation, insets, padding and actions. Width alone cannot establish fit.

For example, a 390-unit-high area with 72 units of top chrome, 56 of actions and
32 of spacing leaves 230 for the task body. A 410-high minimum body cannot fit.
These are not constants to copy. Reallocate structure before reducing text or
targets. Use measured container bounds or complete measurable forms.
`UI.environment(taskBody)` reads an existing GuiObject's size in layout units;
`UI.environment()` reads the camera. Measure the bounded parent that supplies
room, not a hugging child whose size changes with your chosen form. Route:
`environment.environment`.

The [inspection example](../examples/inspection.luau) creates its bounded body
before its panes. Use that body's `viewportHeight` and `viewportWidth` in
record-form formulas.
When a top-art card cannot show its name and decisive values in the remaining
height, try a thumbnail beside those facts. Let column count fall to one when
readable records require it. A virtual collection limits mounted rows; it does
not make a tall row fit. Do not add a permanent Inspect footer when record
activation already opens detail. Keep commitment in that detail step.

Do not force a mid-collection `initialFocus` for every input. It can scroll a
fresh touch browse away from the beginning. Choose focus entry for the active
input; preserve the player's actual key and scroll context on return.

Two possible allocations, not required skins:

```text
PORTRAIT INSPECTION          SHORT INSPECTION
Title / Back                Title / Back
Selected subject            Subject     Name + decisive facts
Name + decisive facts                   Supporting detail scrolls
Supporting detail scrolls               Commit / accepted state
Commit / accepted state
```

A pinned action is useful while its terms and readable content still fit. With
large text or the keyboard, permit an accessible scrolling task surface rather
than overlap or a fixed footer that traps controls.

NavigationSplitView can preserve panes while presenting one compact step;
TabView owns peer selection and adaptive navigation. AdaptiveStack changes a
relationship's axis; it does not decide which facts matter. ViewThatFits needs
bounded, measurable complete candidates; a fill-width candidate always fits.
Use [advanced surfaces](advanced-surfaces.md) for their ownership contracts.

The [collection example](../examples/collection.luau) implements search, type,
sort, active-filter summary, result count, reset and empty recovery. Its bounded
browse keeps search and Filters on one row. Filters opens a separate native
navigation step with Back and Show results, rather than growing above the list.
The retained model keeps query, filters, selection and scroll on return. Wide
records use visual Cards; narrow or short bodies put a thumbnail beside the
name, facts, price and Inspect. Both forms preserve the same decision and model.
Supply the game's records and art; keep commitment in the selected detail.

Opening optional content must give it useful room. In short height, condense the
summary or use a detail step with Back so the requested records are visible.
Disclosure alone does not help if a tall introduction still pushes every record
below the fold. Keep accepted state and a clear route back to the main decision.

## Correct the actual view

Apply the skill's capture correction loop to portrait and short height separately,
on entry and after rotation. The first useful item, recognizable subject and
action with its consequence must survive the smaller form.

Use actual Touch and controller input to confirm native-control clearance,
hold/release/cancel, focus/Back, large text, and rejection. Rotation preserves
selection, drafts and accepted state; unrelated tasks must not inherit scroll
position. Captures do not establish behavior or retained-memory performance.
]]></string>
								<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
								<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
								<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
								<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
								<string name="Name">compact-compositions.md</string>
								<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
								<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
								<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
								<UniqueId name="UniqueId">73c8afe78d24b4d30afced110000080c</UniqueId>
							</Properties>
						</Item>
						<Item class="StringValue" referent="1072">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Game screens

Choose the surface and composition from the task. A game does not need a hub,
shop, inventory, editor or tutorial unless its brief calls for one. The patterns
below are alternatives, not a screen inventory or an application template.
Use [patterns](patterns.md) for complete examples and the control references
for exact options.

## Choose presentation and navigation

| Relationship to the current activity | Starting point |
| --- | --- |
| Monitor or act while play continues | Composition with Regions |
| Inspect optional nearby context | Callout, Popover or DisclosureGroup |
| Complete a bounded modal task | Sheet or Dialog |
| Enter a mode that replaces play | A page with a Screen surface |
| Switch frequently between peer tasks | TabView |
| Go deeper and return | NavigationStack within the chosen surface |
| Browse candidates and inspect a selection | NavigationSplitView or a collection with a separate detail step |

Opening from a HUD does not require a centered Sheet. Decide how much world
context and input access the task needs. A modal owns input; visible gameplay
behind it is not necessarily interactive or paused. Use `suspendGameplay = true`
and gate game commands with `UI.environment().gameplaySuspended` when the app
should suspend them. A Studio plugin uses its host's mounting and input model;
read `Mounting` and [runtime details](runtime-details.md) for that case.

## Navigation and lifetime

Give title and return controls a clear owner. A task root can have Close;
Back returns to a parent step. A root with no parent has no Back. Redundant
controls are useful only when reach, scrolling or the host's conventions need
another access point.

For a Sheet with child navigation, build NavigationStack inside its `content`
so the stack's lifetime belongs to the Sheet. Use a bounded detent, such as
`large`, for a stack that fills its viewport. A single-step task can mount its
body directly. Keep shared selection and drafts outside disposable content.

When closing an animated surface, preserve its content through departure.
Reset a transient path and root on the next opening if the task should start
fresh; preserve them if the brief requires resuming. Clone navigation paths
without replacing unchanged entry tables. Push a child or replace an entry
in one path update rather than calling Back and deferring another navigation.
Do not close and reopen a Sheet merely to change its child step.

Route Back, Close and dismissal through the same dirty-draft policy when
edits could be lost. Read `Sheet.dismissal` and the navigation control's
contract. Keep confirmations distinct from the task being confirmed and
settle them according to the actual accepted result.

Test direct entry, child entry, Back, Close and reopening, including interrupted
transitions. Verify restored focus, state and gameplay input.

## Fit the task body

Measure the containing surface, including height and insets. Use
`UI.environment(container)` for a bounded task, not the world viewport.
Choose pane minimums, column counts and preview sizes from readable content,
useful subject detail and input targets. Sample pixel sizes are not breakpoints
for every game.

A collection may use rows, tiles or a table. Put filters beside, above or in a
separate step according to their complexity and the available room. A wrapping
`HStack { width = "fill", wrap = true }` suits short peer choices;
AdaptiveStack suits a group that should change between a row and a column.
Neither is required for every filter or choice.

Bound scrolling content. Keep commitment and its terms accessible without
trapping them behind a fixed footer when the keyboard or larger text reduces
room. See [compact compositions](compact-compositions.md).

## Choose, inspect and edit

Use [Collection](../examples/collection.luau) for one browse/detail implementation
and [Inspection](../examples/inspection.luau) for a subject-led decision.
Their models, theme assets, field names and controls illustrate those tasks.
Transfer only the parts the current application needs.

Choose a segmented Picker when a few short alternatives fit and benefit from
being visible together. Use Chips with a swatch `leading` for a named palette,
a menu for longer choices, and ColorPicker when arbitrary color entry is needed.
Keep selection distinguishable beyond color. Preview a draft where that helps
predict the accepted result; preserve it on rejected or cancelled work according
to the task's save policy.

For a purchase, show the item and complete terms at commitment. Add confirmation
in proportion to consequence, reversibility and the platform's requirements.
Use emphasis to establish action priority; every affordable item need not have
an equally prominent Buy button. Keep owned, unavailable and selected states
clear when they affect the decision.

## HUD and contextual help

For a course map, radar or spatial overview, use the
[minimap guide](../../../docs/guide/minimaps.md). It combines Region forms,
game-owned drawing, an optional opening action and a full-map destination.

Use Composition Regions for information that needs to coexist with play.
Prioritize from urgency, frequency and the camera's focus area. Provide smaller
complete forms when regions compete for room. Keep required tasks reachable
through any compact menu. Read `HUD-actions` for input ownership and bindings.

Check native touch-control clearance in the actual experience; fixed offsets
from one game's controls do not establish clearance elsewhere. Investigate
`touch-control-overlap` and `composition-unfit` findings with real labels and
supported inputs. Test text against gameplay; use a backing surface when
contrast needs it, rather than requiring a panel behind every readout.

Contextual teaching can use Callout anchored to the live target. Focused reading
may need a different surface. Keep the target usable, provide an exit, and use
real completion evidence when teaching an action.

## Quick wheel example

This example declares a game-owned action for a RadialMenu over play. Its Q and
ButtonY bindings and chat content are illustrative; choose bindings that fit
the application's existing commands. The shortcut hint displays the binding;
the action must also drive the menu.

```luau
local chatAction = Host.InputAction "QuickChat" { Type = Enum.InputActionType.Bool,
    Host.InputBinding { KeyCode = Enum.KeyCode.Q }, Host.InputBinding { KeyCode = Enum.KeyCode.ButtonY } }
Host.InputContext "Commands" { Parent = playerGui, chatAction }
local wheelOpen = C.cell(false)
local items: { Facet.RadialItem<string> } = {}
for index, message in MESSAGES do
    items[index] = { id = "chat" .. index, label = message.short, onSelect = function() sendChat(message.text) end }
end
local chatRegion = UI.Region "Chat" { zone = "right", rank = 3,
    UI.Button "Chat" { icon = "chat", shape = "circle", caption = "Chat", shortcutHint = chatAction,
        onActivate = function() wheelOpen:set(true) end } }
local quickChat = UI.RadialMenu<<string>>({ label = "Quick chat", items = items, launcher = false,
    isPresented = wheelOpen, holdAction = chatAction }, "QuickChat")
```

Mount `chatRegion` in the HUD Composition and `quickChat` beside it in the
same mount. Use concise, distinct labels and test localization. Label width,
item count and available space determine whether the menu can remain radial;
a character-count rule does not prove fit. Inspect `RadialPanel.Presentation`
(`radial` or `list`) after opening. With `launcher = false`, the ring opens at
the screen center. Check its actual fallback and input behavior.

For results and preferences, [Outcome](../examples/outcome.luau) and
[Settings](../examples/settings.luau) demonstrate accepted data and save callbacks.
Neither example grants rewards or implements server acceptance for the game.
]]></string>
