								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="1080">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Layout and navigation

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

## Choose the relationship

Organize the information around what the player must compare and do. Keep
facts used in one decision together. Separate unrelated tasks before adding
panels. The same task can use a different arrangement when space changes,
but its identity, commitment and return route must remain clear.

Choose the body from the decision before writing a shared page helper:

| Player task | Body relationship | Starting controls |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Inspect one object | Prominent subject beside decisive facts and action | Stage, bounded stacks |
| Browse and inspect | Candidates beside selected detail, then a detail step when compact | NavigationSplitView, VirtualList or VirtualGrid |
| Compare shared attributes | Aligned values with a clear selected candidate | Table |
| Edit related values | Labeled groups with current values, consequences and save state | VStack, the appropriate input controls |
| Review a result | Personal outcome first, accepted gains next, comparison after | Stacks, a bounded Table when needed |

Share navigation, input lifetime and visual roles. Let the body choose its own
layout and scrolling boundary. Do not make a helper wrap every destination in
the same scrolling text column. A short choice can fit its content; an inspector
can use available room for the subject. On a wide surface, extra room should
support comparison or inspection, or remain deliberate space around a bounded task.

Use VStack for reading order, HStack for one compact unit and Grid for a small
static arrangement. These containers arrange a chosen relationship; they do not
choose the relationship for the player.

Screen arranges a vertical page with safe margins. It is not an overlay layer.
Mount Composition directly for a HUD. Ordinary interactive screens use device
chrome. Give a page that replaces gameplay `surface = "surface"` on its Screen.
The plate then reaches every edge of the glass, and the text and controls stay
safe. Keep the app's ScreenInsets. Other edge-reaching artwork still needs safe
text and controls.

## Fit without losing the task

Stacks hug by default. Bound the task before fill collections or wrapped text.
On a container, `width = "fill"` spans the parent. A `UI.fill()` child shares
remaining space along its parent stack's main axis. Text and Button do not take
container options such as `width`; use parent stretch or explicit native sizing.
Read `Layout-options` and `Text.card` before sharing a sizing helper.
Use `maxWidth` to keep a reading or decision area
comfortable in a wide viewport. Use a wrapping HStack (`wrap = true`) when
peer items can use several rows, and give it `width = "fill"`: a wrapping row
that hugs never wraps. Use AdaptiveStack when one group should
change between a row and a column. It keeps the same children mounted.

Allocate room to the required decision first. Let optional art, introductory
copy and secondary facts yield before the action and exit. Use ScrollView
for content that can extend beyond a bounded viewport. Keep a selected
object's commitment outside a long browsing collection. Do not let a preview
push that commitment below the shortest viewport.

For a small peer-card grid whose content sets the surface height, use
VirtualGrid with `minColumnWidth`, `mode = "all"` and `hug = true`.
Its container sets the columns and shared row width. Keep a large catalog
in a bounded scrolling collection instead. Read `VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.sizing`
and `VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.mode` for the exact sizing contract.

Adapt to the containing surface, including its height. `UI.environment(source)`
can read a native container's room; a camera viewport is not the width of a
nested panel. Prefer native layout and controls that measure their own room.
Input and viewing distance are separate: a near gamepad player can still use
two readable panes. See [input](INPUT_FOCUS_AND_ACCESS.md) for those decisions.

Composition ranks competing Regions; lower ranks take precedence. Each form
needs a measurable size and a useful complete meaning. A fill-sized form cannot
describe its intrinsic requirement. Use mayDrop only when an immediate required
action has an equally usable alternative. A compact Menu owns its own action
and must actually open the omitted choices.

ViewThatFits tries alternatives in order and still shows the last if none fit.
Its final form must work at the smallest supported room. It does not decide
which information is safe to remove. Give candidates an intrinsic or fixed
size; a fill-width candidate always passes the width check. Candidates are
separate mounted trees, so shared values belong in the model. Create a new
Instance for each candidate rather than parenting the same Instance twice.

## Navigate deliberately

TabView is for stable peer destinations. NavigationStack is for a meaningful
path into detail. NavigationSplitView is for a collection whose selection
determines related detail, not two unrelated panels.

Keep the title, selected section and Back destination clear. Preserve selection,
drafts and useful scroll position on return. In compact split views, deliberate
activation opens detail and Back returns to the collection. A directional
focus move should not unexpectedly replace the collection. Set pane minimums
from readable content rather than forcing two unusable panes. Keep actions in
a stable order across arrangements so players can use what they learned.

NavigationSplitView retains both panes when it changes arrangement. Bind its
`preferredCompactColumn` and set it to `"detail"` when an activation opens the
selected object. Back writes `"sidebar"`. `minSidebarWidth`, `minDetailWidth`
and `compactWidth` express the room needed by the content; `axis = "y"` has
equivalent height options. Use the installed contract for those options.

NavigationStack's writable `path` owns the route. TabView's writable
`selection` owns the peer destination. Both use Compose LayerStack for page
lifetime. Keep durable values outside pages even when visited pages are
retained; TabView can dispose departing pages with `retention = "top"`.
Use Compose branches for changing content inside a page, not a second route
history or an extra application mount.

NavBar arranges navigation chrome but does not own history. Hiding built-in
chrome requires a complete visible return route. A root page has no Back.
Do not turn every small setting adjustment into another page.

When a destination contains compact split navigation, use Close to return to
gameplay and label the inner Back with its collection name. Two controls named
Back must not lead to different places. Give the split's `compactBack` cell to
the page NavBar's `onBack`, and set `detailLabel = false` when an activation
opens the detail, so the page keeps one header. Test the composed page and body
together; their headers and safe margins consume the same limited height.

PageView suits a short inspectable sequence; expose an alternative to swiping.
Pagination suits genuinely bounded result pages. StepIndicator describes task
stages, not destinations. ZoomView preserves spatial relationships in a map or
board; keep global actions outside its zoomed content.

## Check the connected route

Open the task from gameplay, enter detail, change the viewport while it is
open, return and reopen. Check the shortest room with long labels and the
on-screen keyboard. Confirm that the task keeps its state and that focus,
scroll position and gameplay input return to useful places.

Read `Tasks.pages` for the destination and input lifetime, then
`Tasks.collection-detail` or `Tasks.tabs` for its body. Read
`Layout-options`, `fill`, `AdaptiveStack.reference`, `ViewThatFits.reference`
and the chosen navigation control's contract for exact configuration.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">LAYOUT_AND_NAVIGATION.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">73c8afe78d24b4d30afced1100000815</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="1081">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Motion and feedback

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

Motion explains a change: where a page went, what gained focus, which value
changed, or where an accepted object arrived. It must not delay essential input
or make the player follow an animation to learn the result.

Separate immediate input feedback from progress and accepted outcome.
A pressed state can confirm that input arrived while the game still awaits a server result.
Keep the final result readable after motion ends. Reinforce urgent feedback with
more than one signal; sound, haptics or color alone can be missed.

Use theme timing and easing. Controls already provide shared feedback; do not
stack a custom loop on the same property. Use native StyleSheet transitions
where supported and `runtime.tween` or `runtime.spring` for other reactive motion.
Do not create another animation scheduler.

Use `Custom-transitions` for Sheet, TabView and NavigationStack presentation.
Bind through the supplied progress and owner so interruption and cleanup remain coherent.
For other Compose motion, supply a reactive `reducedMotion` policy; Compose core
does not discover the platform preference. Read `Motion.motionLevel` for Facet's policy.

Keep direct manipulation attached to input. After release, motion can explain
arrival or return. Hover and press must not change measured layout, move hit
targets or clip the emphasized control. Keep text readable during transitions.

Use Travel for an object moving between meaningful locations and `UI.sequence`
for ordered presentation. Keep accepted ownership in the model, independent
of either animation. Skip, interruption, navigation away and reduced motion
must preserve the same final state and cleanup.
Read `Effect-sequences` and `Travel.reference` for their contracts.

Use `UI.shake(faces, { intensity = 1 })` for brief rejection feedback.
Pass existing tile faces inside stable layout slots and call the handle's
`play()` method. The effect restores their rotations on completion, cancel,
replay and cleanup. Limited and none motion keep the faces still.
Keep the rejection reason and next action visible after the effect ends.

Reduced motion can execute sequence callbacks immediately. Do not depend on
a duration to grant rewards, release input or complete a task. Repeated state
updates should retarget or settle coherently rather than accumulating effects.

Test rapid reversal, repeat activation, resizing, theme changes, removal,
navigation during motion and reduced motion. At a fixed viewport, an idle
surface must settle. Measure frame cost and retained resources across repeated
navigation, not only during one quiet frame.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">MOTION_AND_FEEDBACK.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">73c8afe78d24b4d30afced1100000816</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="1082">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Surfaces and feedback

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

## Decide what interrupts

Use the least interruption that makes the decision clear. Consider urgency,
consequence and whether the player must keep watching or controlling the world.
Keep information at its source when it helps the current task. Take attention
only when proceeding without a decision would create a meaningful problem.

DisclosureGroup expands optional facts in the reading flow. Popover attaches
a quick task to its source. Callout teaches a contextual action. Alert confirms
a brief consequential decision. Dialog or Sheet owns a bounded interruption.
Recurring destinations generally need pages.

Separate required facts from optional detail. A price, risk or reason an action
is unavailable must not depend on hover. Progressive disclosure should reduce
distraction while keeping the next step and its consequence discoverable.
For example, an optional stat breakdown can expand beside an upgrade, while
its cost and effect stay beside the commitment.

A modal does not automatically pause the game or suppress custom gameplay
commands. Define input ownership, visible exits and recovery. Do not stack
modals to simulate navigation.

Popover's default `compact = "sheet"` adapts an anchored task for narrow touch
room. Use the control's route change and keep durable state in the model.
The same task needs a clear title, commitment and exit in either presentation.

For a floating Sheet, center placement has a visible Close and no grabber.
Edge docking serves tasks where resizing or edge dismissal helps. Leave
closeButton absent to let the exit follow placement. Use hug for short intrinsic
content; bound and scroll collections. Keep actions reachable at every detent.
Apply Sheet.dismissal to all dirty exits. Pinned actions do not close it
automatically; rejected work must remain usable.

Alert dismisses before invoking its action. Use a controlled Dialog or Sheet
when the task must remain open for an accepted or rejected request. Read the
chosen contract before wiring dismissal or pending behavior.

Dialog reads `isPresented` and requests changes through `onPresentedChange`;
the model accepts or refuses the request. Its actions propose closure by
default; `keepOpen = true` suppresses that proposal. Sheet actions never close
the sheet. Use `Sheet.dismissal` for its guard instead of copying Dialog's
action options. Keep one dismissal policy for Close, Back, outside presses,
gestures and actions that leave a dirty task.

## Explain state where it matters

Use Notice for persistent state, Toast for a brief acknowledgement, Badge or
StatusIndicator for a compact attached signal. Keep required consequences
available after a toast expires. Aggregate repeated gains and remove messages
whose context no longer applies.
Do not dismiss the only explanation for a blocked action while it remains
blocked. An expiring Undo must not be the only way to prevent serious loss.

Put feedback near the object or action that changed. State what happened and
what the player can do next. Match emphasis to urgency; repeated routine
success should not compete with danger or the player's current decision.
Use a confirmation only when it helps the player understand or prevent a
meaningful error. Name the object and consequence in its action labels.

ProgressView shows a meaningful quantity or unknown wait. Timer displays a
deadline; zero does not independently authorize rewards or completion.
Skeleton reserves known loading structure. Keep loading separate from an
empty result. Show progress only from a known measure; use an unknown wait
when the game cannot measure completion. Retrying must not create a second
purchase or repeat an already accepted action.

ErrorBoundary contains failures while building or updating its UI region.
Its `fallback(failure, retry)` rebuilds that region when retried. A rejected
server request is model state, not a rendering failure. Show its explanation
and recovery through normal bound content, using Compose.show or Compose.switch
when the structure changes. Keep useful input available in either case.

## Give content an intentional frame

Use semantic Text roles, readable contrast and bounded wrapping. Text and
important actions must survive missing art, long labels and larger text.
ShortcutHint describes an actual binding; it does not create one.

Image and AsyncImage need stable dimensions and a useful failure state.
Stage should frame the object clearly at a useful scale; inspection controls
must not also activate the surrounding item. AvatarGroup summarizes membership;
use a list when players must compare individual status.
Use a static image when it conveys the same decision at lower cost. Create
expensive previews only when useful; keep stable space while they load.

Typewriter needs a finish action distinct from advancing dialogue. Reduced
motion and Skip still reveal the complete result. Keep accepted state
independent of presentation timing.

## Verify interruption and return

Open from a meaningful source, act, cancel, reject a request and reopen. Check
that feedback describes the accepted state, the previous task remains usable,
and focus returns to the source or a useful surviving target. Change room and
input while the surface is open. Test the visible exit and Back in every
presentation, including a short viewport with the keyboard open.

Read `Tasks.sheet`, `Sheet.layout`, `Sheet.dismissal`, `Dialog.Dialog`,
`Alert.reference` and `Popover.compact` for presentation ownership. Read
`ErrorBoundary.reference` and `Typewriter.reference` for their recovery and
completion contracts.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">SURFACES_INFORMATION_AND_FEEDBACK.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">73c8afe78d24b4d30afced1100000817</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
						</Item>
						<Item class="StringValue" referent="1083">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Extend a task with helpers

Use a standard control when it already owns the behavior. Read only the route
for the needed extension; retrieve these node names with the docs command.
Helpers are not a checklist of features to add to a screen.

| Need | Helper and exact route | Boundary to preserve |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Read room, input or access preferences | `environment` | Layout follows available room; input prompts follow active input. These are separate values. A container source differs from the viewport. Readables end with their owner. |
| Fill remaining stack space | `fill` | Bound the main axis first. Fill is not a generic leaf width or a guarantee that a label fits. Use stack stretch for the cross axis. |
| Mark an existing object | `badged` | Keep the host's hit target. Leave room for a badge painted beyond its bounds. Use a readable count or dot, not essential long text. |
| Enter a passive HUD with a controller | `responder` | Composition already has a responder. Configure that owner instead of adding another. End engagement and restore gameplay on return. |
| Group native focus | `focusSection` | This groups existing targets; it adds neither a target nor commands. Choose entry and return policy. Do not steal focus after the player moved elsewhere. |
| Style focus outside app mounting | `focusRing` | `app.mount` installs this already. Respect custom selection images and dispose the owner. A visual ring does not establish navigation order. |
| Apply commands to keyed native targets | `commandScope` → `Focus-and-command-scopes` | Keys and nodes must be unique. Text entry and more specific modal/control actions win. The scope does not replace directional navigation. |
| Adjust a custom value digitally | `adjustable` | Use one nonselectable readout as the stop, with no selectable descendants. Keep touch/pointer actions outside it. Define allowed directions at limits. Prefer Slider or Stepper for their ordinary tasks. |
| Move data between owned targets | `draggable`, `dropTarget` → `draggable-and-dropTarget` | A gesture is intent. For asynchronous acceptance return `pending`, then call `complete(true)` and update the model only if it succeeds. Cancelled or stale completion must not move data. Provide controller and touch routes. |
| Resolve a custom gesture conflict | `gestureArbiter` | It makes decisions from events you supply; it creates no input listeners. Prefer the standard control's arbitration. Dispose the gesture session on cancellation. |
| Reveal a result in an existing scroller | `scrollTo`, `scrollToVisible` → `scrollTo-and-scrollToVisible` | Call after a meaningful event, not every frame. A false return can mean no movement was needed. Preserve reduced motion and nested scrolling. |
| Attach custom UI to a world target | `worldAnchor` | A projection is not game permission or proximity validation. Define hidden, missing, offscreen and occluded states. The owner releases projection work. Prefer WorldMarker for a standard marker. |
| Explain an accepted change | `sequence`, `countUp` → `Effect-sequences` | Theme tokens own timing. Cancellation or reduced motion must preserve the accepted result. Decorative completion must not grant rewards or save state. |
| Reinforce a rejected action | `shake` → `Effect-sequences` | Shake stable inner faces and retain the rejection reason. The effect restores rotations; do not combine competing rotation owners. |
| Reinforce an accepted action | `feedback` | Haptics supplement a visible or audible result. They cannot be the only error, success or navigation signal. |

For a custom interaction, test entry, activation, cancellation, limits and return
with each supported input. Check that disposal releases commands, subscriptions
and gesture state. A resize or hidden form must not erase a draft or duplicate
a subscription. `ViewThatFits` keeps its candidates mounted; use shared state
and avoid repeated side effects in the alternative forms.
]]></string>
